<h2>1. Summary</h2>

<p>
{{ tool_name }} is an open-source command-line tool that {{ applicant_legal_entity }} uses to manage
<strong>its own Google Ads accounts</strong> declaratively from version-controlled <code>.bid</code>
files. It is &ldquo;Terraform for Google Ads&rdquo;: campaigns, ad groups, ads, keywords, budgets, and
conversion actions are described in source files; a <code>plan</code> step diffs that declaration
against the live account; an <code>apply</code> step prints the diff, asks the operator to confirm,
and only then issues the change.
</p>

<p>
{{ tool_name }} is in <strong>production use</strong> at {{ applicant_legal_entity }} across its live
campaigns and accounts. It is single-tenant: it manages only {{ applicant_legal_entity }}&rsquo;s own
accounts under one manager (MCC) account. It is not multi-tenant, is not sold or offered to third
parties, authenticates no end users, and reads or writes no end-user data. Its permissible use is
<strong>ad creation and management, and reporting</strong>.
</p>

<p>
Section 2 describes {{ applicant_legal_entity }}&rsquo;s business and where {{ tool_name }} sits in
it; §3 explains why Google Ads API access is functionally necessary for that business.
</p>

<h2>2. Business model</h2>

{% for paragraph in core_business_paragraphs %}<p>{{ paragraph }}</p>
{% endfor %}

<p><strong>Primary customers:</strong> {{ primary_customers }}</p>

<p><strong>Value exchange:</strong> {{ value_exchange }}</p>

<p>
{{ tool_name }} sits outside that value exchange. It is not the product, is not sold, licensed, or
offered to anyone, and no transaction of any kind occurs &ldquo;on&rdquo; it. Google Ads is one of
the marketing channels through which {{ applicant_legal_entity }} reaches the customers described
above, and {{ tool_name }} is the internal tool its team uses to operate that channel safely.
</p>

<h2>3. Why the Google Ads API is essential</h2>

{% for paragraph in why_this_tool_exists_paragraphs %}<p>{{ paragraph }}</p>
{% endfor %}

<p>
That workflow exists only on top of the API. Each capability it depends on maps to a specific API
service, and none of them has an equivalent in the Google Ads web interface or in Google Ads Editor:
</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Capability the workflow depends on</th><th>API service</th><th>Why nothing else substitutes</th></tr>
  {% for n in api_necessity %}<tr><td>{{ n.capability | safe }}</td><td>{{ n.service | safe }}</td><td>{{ n.why | safe }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>

<p>
<strong>How API access directly improves the service:</strong> every change to a live campaign is
authored as text, reviewed by a second person in a Git pull request, validated end-to-end by
Google&rsquo;s own <code>validateOnly</code> pass, and applied as one atomic batch under a named
operator&rsquo;s confirmation. That turns budget-affecting campaign edits from ad-hoc UI clicks into
audited, peer-reviewed, reversible changes: fewer erroneous spend changes, a complete audit trail in
both Git and the Google Ads change history, and campaign operations that stay reliable as the account
grows. {{ tool_name }} has no fallback if API access is removed — it does not scrape the web
interface or automate a browser; the plan/apply workflow described in §4 cannot be built on anything
except these endpoints.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Who uses it:</strong> {{ who_uses_it_operators }}. Changes to <code>.bid</code> files are
reviewed in Git pull requests, the same way as application code. No third party, end user, or external
advertiser has access to the tool or to the credentials it carries — the OAuth credentials are
{{ applicant_legal_entity }}&rsquo;s own and there is no &ldquo;manage someone else&rsquo;s
account&rdquo; capability.
</p>

<div class="callout">
<strong>Access classification: internal tool.</strong> {{ tool_name }} is used only by
{{ applicant_legal_entity }}&rsquo;s own employees and contractors, on
{{ applicant_legal_entity }}&rsquo;s own accounts. It is not accessible to
{{ applicant_legal_entity }}&rsquo;s customers, clients, or any other external user, and there is no
hosted or multi-tenant deployment of it.
</div>

<h2>4. How it works</h2>

<p>
{{ tool_name }} is a single Rust binary with no server component and no background processes. Every
Google Ads API call is initiated by a human operator running a command on their own workstation —
there is no scheduler, webhook, or automated trigger. The central design property is the boundary
between read and write:
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Read / dry-run</strong> — <code>validate</code> (local only), <code>plan</code>,
      <code>query</code>, <code>pull</code>, <code>refresh</code>, and <code>drift</code> never mutate
      the account.
      <code>plan</code> fetches live state, diffs it against the <code>.bid</code> files, and runs the
      resulting mutate with <code>validateOnly=true</code>, so Google validates references, enums,
      policy strings, and field limits without writing anything. <code>drift</code> is read-only
      metadata plus reporting: it asks <code>GoogleAdsFieldService</code> which fields a resource
      exposes, reads the public discovery document to tell settable fields from output-only ones,
      and then selects the fields <code>plan</code> does not — so the operator can see what the
      diff is silent about rather than assuming an <code>unchanged</code> row covers everything.</li>
  <li><strong>Write</strong> — only <code>apply</code> writes, and only after it prints the same diff
      and the operator confirms by typing literally <code>yes</code>. If the
      <code>validateOnly</code> pass rejected anything, the real mutate is skipped — partial
      application is not possible. <code>apply</code> refuses to prompt when stdin is not a TTY; an
      explicit <code>--auto-approve</code> flag is required in a trusted CI context.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout">
No Google Ads resource is ever mutated without an explicit operator confirmation (or an explicit
<code>--auto-approve</code> flag on a trusted, non-TTY environment). This read/write boundary is the
core safety property of the tool.
</div>

<p>
All writes in one <code>apply</code> go to <code>googleAds:mutate</code> as a single atomic batch,
ordered by resource dependency (budgets &rarr; campaigns &rarr; ad groups &rarr; ads &rarr; criteria),
so either every operation in the batch succeeds or none are written. The one exception is the custom
audience, which <code>GoogleAdsService.Mutate</code> has no operation type for: those go first, in a
separate <code>customAudiences:mutate</code> call, and the batch then references the resource names it
returned. Both calls run under <code>validateOnly</code> before either is committed. Every change to a
<code>.bid</code> file is a reviewable Git commit, and the Google Ads change-history view records the
same operations under the operator&rsquo;s identity. Parse, schema, and policy errors render with file
path, line number, and a source snippet (via the <code>miette</code> diagnostic library), so a
validation failure traces straight back to the <code>.bid</code> line that caused it.
</p>

<h2>5. Google Ads API usage</h2>

<p>
{{ tool_name }} uses the <strong>Google Ads REST API</strong> over HTTPS. The endpoint version is
pinned via the <code>BIDSMITH_API_VERSION</code> environment variable (currently <code>{{ api_version }}</code>).
It calls these endpoints:
</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Endpoint</th><th>Method</th><th>Used by</th><th>validateOnly</th></tr>
  {% for e in endpoints %}<tr><td><code>{{ e.endpoint }}</code></td><td>{{ e.method }}</td><td>{{ e.used_by }}</td><td>{{ e.validate_only }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>

<p>
Reads use <code>googleAds:searchStream</code> with {{ gaql_query_count }} fixed GAQL queries — one per
resource type {{ tool_name }} models, plus the account row itself for its
currency ({% for q in gaql_queries %}<code>{{ q.label }}</code>{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}) — plus the read-only <code>query</code> passthrough for
ad-hoc reporting and the read-only <code>keyword-ideas</code> research call
(<code>KeywordPlanIdeaService.GenerateKeywordIdeas</code>). Writes use <code>googleAds:mutate</code> — plus
<code>CustomAudienceService.MutateCustomAudiences</code> for the one resource type that batch cannot
carry — as described in §4.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Operation volume.</strong> A typical production day is {{ volume_typical_per_day }}; on a busy
day it reaches {{ volume_ceiling }} — well within the Basic Access limit of 15,000 operations per day.
</p>

