<h2>7. Authentication and data handling</h2>

<p>
{{ tool_name }} uses the standard Google Ads credential set — a developer token, an OAuth 2.0
desktop-app client owned by {{ applicant_legal_entity }}&rsquo;s Google Cloud project, and a refresh
token issued to a named {{ applicant_legal_entity }} employee for the <code>adwords</code> scope —
supplied either via environment variables or a local <code>~/.bidsmith/credentials.toml</code> (file
mode 0600) written by the tool&rsquo;s <code>auth login</code> command, which runs a one-time
browser-based OAuth authorization-code flow (loopback redirect + PKCE) to obtain the refresh token.
On each invocation it exchanges the refresh token for a short-lived
access token at <code>https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token</code>; the access token lives only in process
memory for the duration of the run and is never written to disk or logged. The only OAuth scope
requested is <code>{{ oauth_scope }}</code> — no Drive, Gmail, Calendar, or Cloud Platform scopes.
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Read from Google Ads:</strong> {{ applicant_legal_entity }}&rsquo;s own account
      configuration and reporting metrics — campaigns, ad groups, ads, keywords, budgets, conversion
      actions, and assets.</li>
  <li><strong>Write to Google Ads:</strong> declarative updates to those same resources, originating
      from <code>.bid</code> files an operator authored and reviewed.</li>
  <li><strong>Stored locally:</strong> the <code>.bid</code> source files (in Git) and a small
      <code>.bidsmith/</code> on-disk cache of the most recent live-state read, used to make
      <code>plan</code> fast. The cache holds only account configuration the operator can already see;
      it never contains credentials.</li>
  <li><strong>Stored locally (credentials):</strong> when <code>auth login</code> is used, the refresh
      and developer tokens are written to <code>~/.bidsmith/credentials.toml</code> at file mode 0600;
      environment-variable setups persist no credentials at all.</li>
  <li><strong>Never touched:</strong> end-user PII, third-party advertiser data, or any Google data
      beyond what OAuth requires. Network egress is limited to <code>oauth2.googleapis.com</code> and
      <code>googleads.googleapis.com</code> over HTTPS.</li>
</ul>

